WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.199
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:08.468
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.768 --> 00:00:15.878
Unit five: Consciousness and AI. In this unit, you will debate whether machines could ever be conscious.

4
00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:19.138
artificial general intelligence.

5
00:00:19.838 --> 00:00:27.132
artificial general intelligence means a hypothetical AI able to perform any intellectual task a human can.

6
00:00:27.432 --> 00:00:32.636
Experts disagree about when, or whether, artificial general intelligence will arrive.

7
00:00:33.336 --> 00:00:37.463
Your turn. Say: artificial general intelligence.

8
00:00:40.163 --> 00:00:42.325
artificial general intelligence.

9
00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:45.084
machine consciousness.

10
00:00:45.784 --> 00:00:51.197
machine consciousness means the idea that a machine could have subjective experience.

11
00:00:51.497 --> 00:00:55.308
Machine consciousness remains a philosophical puzzle.

12
00:00:56.008 --> 00:00:59.879
Your turn. Say: machine consciousness.

13
00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:04.579
machine consciousness.

14
00:01:05.583 --> 00:01:06.747
Turing test.

15
00:01:07.447 --> 00:01:14.032
Turing test means a test of whether a machine's conversation is indistinguishable from a human's.

16
00:01:14.332 --> 00:01:18.341
Some chatbots now pass simple versions of the Turing test.

17
00:01:19.041 --> 00:01:21.995
Your turn. Say: Turing test.

18
00:01:24.695 --> 00:01:25.882
Turing test.

19
00:01:26.883 --> 00:01:27.977
Chinese room.

20
00:01:28.677 --> 00:01:34.497
Chinese room means Searle's thought experiment arguing that symbol manipulation is not understanding.

21
00:01:34.797 --> 00:01:38.874
The Chinese room challenges claims that computers understand language.

22
00:01:39.574 --> 00:01:42.575
Your turn. Say: Chinese room.

23
00:01:45.275 --> 00:01:46.218
Chinese room.

24
00:01:47.217 --> 00:01:48.287
sentience.

25
00:01:48.987 --> 00:01:53.030
sentience means the capacity to feel sensations and emotions.

26
00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:56.305
Animal welfare laws are based on sentience.

27
00:01:57.005 --> 00:01:59.356
Your turn. Say: sentience.

28
00:02:02.056 --> 00:02:03.057
sentience.

29
00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.411
alignment problem.

30
00:02:06.111 --> 00:02:11.268
alignment problem means the challenge of making AI goals match human values.

31
00:02:11.568 --> 00:02:15.611
The alignment problem worries many AI researchers.

32
00:02:16.311 --> 00:02:19.370
Your turn. Say: alignment problem.

33
00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:23.338
alignment problem.

34
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:25.850
emergent behaviour.

35
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.814
emergent behaviour means complex behaviour arising from simple parts.

36
00:02:31.114 --> 00:02:34.530
Large models show emergent behaviour nobody programmed.

37
00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:38.695
Your turn. Say: emergent behaviour.

38
00:02:41.395 --> 00:02:42.965
emergent behaviour.

39
00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:45.508
large language model.

40
00:02:46.208 --> 00:02:50.600
large language model means an AI trained on vast text to generate language.

41
00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:54.153
A large language model can write essays in seconds.

42
00:02:54.853 --> 00:02:58.457
Your turn. Say: large language model.

43
00:03:01.157 --> 00:03:02.611
large language model.

44
00:03:03.617 --> 00:03:05.802
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:06.102 --> 00:03:11.213
Rithy, do you think a large language model actually understands what it says?

46
00:03:12.517 --> 00:03:15.259
Well, it's not exactly bad at pretending.

47
00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:21.188
That's a lovely bit of understatement. Searle would say it's just a Chinese room.

48
00:03:22.483 --> 00:03:27.980
Manipulating symbols without meaning. Rather like some students before exams!

49
00:03:29.283 --> 00:03:33.155
Ha! But emergent behaviour does surprise researchers.

50
00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:38.333
True. Passing the Turing test isn't the same as sentience, though.

51
00:03:39.617 --> 00:03:42.615
And if we ever built artificial general intelligence?

52
00:03:43.917 --> 00:03:47.913
Then the alignment problem would be, shall we say, slightly important.

53
00:03:49.217 --> 00:03:53.643
Grammar focus: Irony, understatement and tone.

54
00:03:53.943 --> 00:03:58.566
At C2, you can use irony and understatement to add subtle humour.

55
00:03:58.866 --> 00:04:04.235
It's not exactly bad at pretending. The problem would be, shall we say, slightly important.

56
00:04:04.535 --> 00:04:07.173
The real meaning comes from tone and context.

57
00:04:07.473 --> 00:04:14.825
Understatement and irony are typical of educated English humour; context and tone make the real meaning clear.

58
00:04:15.817 --> 00:04:18.724
Now read and listen. Can a Machine Think?.

59
00:04:19.024 --> 00:04:26.991
In 1950, Alan Turing proposed that if a machine could converse like a human, we might call it intelligent.

60
00:04:28.283 --> 00:04:34.091
Decades later, large language models produce fluent text that often passes such informal tests.

61
00:04:35.383 --> 00:04:41.249
Yet philosopher John Searle's Chinese room argument suggests that fluency is not understanding.

62
00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:49.043
A person following rules to manipulate Chinese symbols, he argued, need not understand Chinese.

63
00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:56.645
Machine consciousness raises an even harder question: could a system ever have subjective experience?

64
00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:03.943
Some researchers point to emergent behaviour as evidence that we do not fully understand these systems.

65
00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:10.756
If artificial general intelligence were achieved, the alignment problem would become urgent.

66
00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:18.241
It is, to put it mildly, a question worth answering before the technology answers it for us.

67
00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:22.040
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:25.396
The capacity to feel sensations is blank.

69
00:05:25.696 --> 00:05:31.044
A, sentience. B, alignment problem. C, Turing test.

70
00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.131
The answer is A: sentience.

71
00:05:38.617 --> 00:05:41.742
'It was not exactly cheap' most likely means blank.

72
00:05:42.042 --> 00:05:47.124
A, it was cheap. B, it was expensive. C, it was free.

73
00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:54.033
The answer is B: it was expensive.

74
00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:57.805
Which is an example of understatement?

75
00:05:58.105 --> 00:06:05.149
A, It's a disaster!. B, It's slightly inconvenient (about a flood).. C, It's terrible..

76
00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:13.622
The answer is B: It's slightly inconvenient (about a flood)..

77
00:06:15.117 --> 00:06:18.834
Searle's argument against computer understanding is the blank.

78
00:06:19.134 --> 00:06:24.425
A, Chinese room. B, Turing test. C, large language model.

79
00:06:28.417 --> 00:06:30.973
The answer is A: Chinese room.

80
00:06:32.483 --> 00:06:37.084
According to the text, what would become urgent if AGI were achieved?

81
00:06:37.384 --> 00:06:42.941
A, the Turing test. B, the alignment problem. C, translation.

82
00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.890
The answer is B: the alignment problem.

83
00:06:51.383 --> 00:06:56.834
Now it's your turn. Part one: Could a machine ever truly understand language?

84
00:07:02.134 --> 00:07:08.499
Part two: Take a position, refer to one thought experiment, and add a touch of understatement.

85
00:07:14.799 --> 00:07:27.009
I'm sceptical. The Chinese room suggests that producing correct answers is not the same as understanding them. Current models are, to put it mildly, impressive mimics, but mimicry is not meaning.

86
00:07:27.309 --> 00:07:30.167
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:31.183 --> 00:07:36.750
Great work today! You learned eight new words and irony, understatement and tone.

88
00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:41.827
Today's value is curiosity. Ask questions and find out.

89
00:07:42.127 --> 00:07:44.744
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

90
00:07:45.044 --> 00:07:47.998
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

